-- The Interpretation Act 1978 (1978 c. 30), Schedule 1: words and expressions defined, as a defined-term table.
--
-- THIS FILE IS GENERATED by tools/gen_schedule1.py from tools/schedule1.tsv, which was read against the text by tools/gen_schedule1.py check.
-- Edit the tsv and regenerate; do not edit this file by hand.
--
-- Source: the converter-made plain-text rendering in ../../registers/source-bundle/ukpga-1978-30.txt, not the official text (see SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
--
-- WHAT THE TABLE HOLDS. One constructor of `Schedule 1 entry` per entry of the Schedule, and one per limb of an entry whose limbs are
-- territorial (County court (a) for England and Wales, (b) for Northern Ireland, each with its own bracket), plus one for each of the three
-- paragraph groups at the end of the Schedule. `Schedule 1 — the definition of` returns what the Act states for the entry: the terms it
-- defines, how it defines them (means, includes, points at another enactment), what it is "in relation to", the entry's words as they stand,
-- the enactment or provision it points at, and the bracket at its end.
-- An entry that points at another Act is not worked out here: the pointer is the answer, and the borrowed term is asked as an input by any rule that needs it.
-- The structured entries (British Islands, Colony, Person, Land and so on) have functions of their own in uk-interpretation-definitions.l4.
--
-- WHAT THE BRACKET DOES. A year or date at the end of an entry is read by Schedule 2 para 4(1) (uk-interpretation-application.l4), which applies it to
-- Acts passed before the commencement only (fork F14). `section 5 construes the word in the enactment according to Schedule 1` puts the table, section 5,
-- Schedule 2 para 4 and Schedule 2 Part II together.
--
-- A boolean or a value named "... states ..." is the Schedule's own words; it is not a reading of them.

